2 CONT See a Sample  |  Sign Up Now 
2 CONT
2 CONT He became the chief of the Office of International Relations in 1969. He shepherded guest scientific workers from European, Asian, Middle-Eastern and South American countries and Australia while they worked at the standards agency
2 CONC . He also took the scientists on weekend excursions to experience U.S. life and each year hosted an eclectic Christmas/holiday party that bridged cultures and religions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Described as opinionated but open-minded, Mr. Peiser was the principal liaison officer at NIST for the State Department, the United Nations and other international-related organizations interested in the physical sciences. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was committed to sharing the benefits of metrology -- the science of measurements -- with developing economies throughout the world. He traveled to more than 60 countries, mostly to help them establish sound measurement capabil
2 CONC ities to foster industrial growth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Much of his early work at the institute focused on crystallography, which he believed was at the center of all materials science and engineering. "Thus crystallography has become a prominent key to a widening scientific understand
2 CONC ing of our material universe, and even the life within it," he wrote in 2001 in the agency's Journal of Research. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Peiser wrote more than 130 scientific papers and was honored by many scientific organizations. The 1955 book "X-ray Diffraction by Polycrystalline Materials," of which he was an editor, was the only work that covered defect an
2 CONC d microstructure analysis for many years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was among the first to point out that "the most interesting properties of crystals were not those associated with the physical, chemical, and structural properties of the 'ideally perfect' crystal, but were caused by characteri
2 CONC stic crystal imperfections." 
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired officially from NIST in 1979, but continued scientific work until his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herbert Steffen Peiser was born in Grunewald, Germany, and spent his early childhood in nearby Berlin and Zurich. He studied math, chemistry, and physics at St. John's College at Cambridge University, receiving a bachelor's degree
2 CONC  in 1939 and a master's degree in chemistry in 1943. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the outbreak of World War II, he traveled the "underground railroad" between Italy and Switzerland, helping women and children who were being persecuted for political and religious reasons to reach neutral Switzerland. During t
2 CONC he D-Day invasion of Europe, he provided logistical support to the British army, as well as surveillance activities to support heavy equipment and tank movements on the Normandy beaches. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After World War II, he worked for a steel manufacturer in Sheffield, England. In the 1950s, he was invited to the United States as a visiting scientist for a one-year tenure at the standards bureau. He later joined the agency. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Peiser was an avid bicyclist and won six medals in the Maryland Senior Olympics, in which he rode in the 10- and 20-mile races as recently as 1998. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a longtime member of the Cosmos Club, which he enjoyed for lectures and playing chess and bridge. He also enjoyed collecting stamps and coins, as well as jigsaw and mathematical puzzles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved from Rockville to Asbury Methodist Village about seven years ago. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife of 56 years, Primrose Peiser, died Feb. 16. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include three daughters, Clare Goodman of Rockville, Georgie Dreibelbis of Atglen, Pa., and Alison Kretser Salisbury; a sister; and six grandchildren. 
