1 NAME Isaac S. D. /Sassoon/
2 GIVN Isaac S. D.
2 SURN Sassoon
2 _MARNM Sassoon
2 NICK Chacham Sasoon
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1946
2 ADDR
3 CITY Hitchin
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 TITL R' Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaac S.D. Sassoon is an observant Sephardic rabbi (hakham) and educator. Hakham Sassoon was born into the Sassoon family of London. His initial education was under the tutelage of his father, the renowned scholar 
2 CONC Rabbi Solomon David Sassoon, Hakham Yosef Doury, and others. Later studies were at the prestigious Gateshead Yeshiva and various yeshivoth in Israel. He holds a Ph.D. in Literature from the University of Lisbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Continuing his family's tradition of academic achievement, Hakham Sassoon has published on topics ranging from Scriptural commentary[1] and history, to issues of current concern to the Jewish community [1]. Hakham Sassoon teaches 
2 CONC at the Institute of Traditional Judaism-The Metivta and lectures widely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Highly esteemed in the Sephardic community, Hakham Sassoon has contributed to the maintenance of the heritage of the Babylonian Jewish community [2] and has provided the Syrian Jewish community with an updated and corrected siddur
2 CONC  (Jewish prayer book) reflecting that community's traditions. He has recently published a book called "The Status of Women in Jewish Tradition" addressing the particular religious tradition's outlook on women. He primarily refers 
2 CONC to the Tora, Talmudh, and Dead Sea Scrolls in exploring the authentic position on the "continuum of patriarchal condescension." His goal, in writing the book, was to give authentic answers to a popular (if not hackneyed) question.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selected works
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Status of Women in Jewish Tradition," Cambridge University Press (2011)
2 CONT * Destination Torah: Notes and Reflections on Selected Verses from the Weekly Torah Readings, a commentary on Chumash (Hebrew language edition Ve-ha'arev Na), ISBN 0881256390 ISBN 0-88125-639-0
2 CONT * Uriel da Costa, Examination of Pharisaic Traditions, ed. and trans. with Herman Prins Salomon, Brill, Leiden, (1993) [orig. ed. Amsterdam, 1623]
2 CONT * The Marrano Factory: The Portuguese Inquistion and Its New Christians 1536-1765, Antonio Jose Sariava (Author), Herman Prins Salomon (Translator), I. S. D. Sassoon (Translator), Leiden, 2001. ISBN 9004120807
2 CONT * Siddur Ve-ha'arev Na (2007), a prayerbook according to the Syrian nusach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Articles
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Relevance for Today of Uriel Da Costa's 'Examination of Pharisaic Traditions' , Studia Rosenthaliana, vol. 28, no. 1 (1994)
2 CONT * Et Lirpoh (A Time to Heal), Hadoar, vol. 82, no. 4 (2004).
2 CONT * Review essay [3] on Agunot entitled Ra'ah Ma'aseh Ve'Nizkar Halakhah, Judaism, vol. 54, nos. 1-2 (2005)
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT * Torah Readings De Lange The Expository Times.2003; 114: 177
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_S.D._Sassoon
