1 OCCU Art gallery owner
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Giacomo was a mohel in Paris.He was among the founders of the Chevrat Shas and of the 'Oratoire Ohel Avraham' which became known as the 'rue Cadet shul'. He celebrated a 'Siyum Shas' in Paris in 1867.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giacomo founded an art gallery;" Galerie Tedesco" in 1833, the same year he married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giacomo died during the siege of Paris, 1870, during the Franco-Prussian War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT * Individual: Yechezkel Lifschitz - Lifschitz Web Site
2 CONT * MyHeritage.com family tree
2 CONT * Family site: Lifschitz Web Site
2 CONT * Family tree: LIFSCHITZ - Giacomo Tedesco (Smart Matching)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT First buried in Paris; later in Israel
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jta.org/2013/10/30/news-opinion/israel-middle-east/paris-jew-reburied-in-israel-after-surprise-exhumation
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris Jew’s reburial in Israel ends legal battle
2 CONT
2 CONT October 30, 2013 7:44am
2 CONT
2 CONT (JTA) — A Jewish celebrity art dealer who died in 1870 in France was reburied in Israel after his body was exhumed from his Paris grave due to property laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral for Jacob Giacomo Tedesco on Sunday in Beit Shemesh marked the end of a seven-year legal fight led by his relatives to receive his remains for reburial. Authorities exhumed his body under a French law that allows grave
2 CONC s to be emptied 99 years after a burial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tedesco’s great-great-granddaughter discovered in 2006 that his remains had been exhumed from his grave in the Montpar­nasse Cemetery and placed at the Pere Lachaise depository.
2 CONT
2 CONT “I found it had simply disappeared,” Debby Lifchitz, an Orthodox Jewish woman from Israel, was quoted as telling the Le Figaro newspaper of Tedesco’s grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family then launched the legal battle to have his remains transferred to Israel, “where he would have an eternal resting place,” she was quoted as saying.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tedesco, the owner of a major art gallery, also opened the first kosher butcher shop in Paris, founded an Orthodox synagogue and built a ritual bath that remained active until World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Orthodox Jewish laws dictate Jewish graves be left undisturbed, except for unusual cases.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2011, France passed a law that allows authorities to cremate human remains they exhumed — a practice that also goes against Jewish customs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pinchas Goldschmidt, president of the Conference of European Rabbis, who attended Tedesco’s funeral in Israel, told the Israeli news site Ynet that the French laws on cremation and exhumation should be changed, as they “threaten t
2 CONC o desecrate the dignity of the dead.”
