0 @N6000000006374281683@ NOTE Jewish cemetery Rotenburg an der Fulda
1 CONT
1 CONT Here rests 
1 CONT ein hochbetagter Mann, satt an Jahren; a very old man, tired of years; 
1 CONT sein Name ist Jakob, Sohn des hochverehrten Rabbi His name is Jacob's, hochverehrten son of Rabbi 
1 CONT Meier Wertheim aus R"B. Meier Wertheim from R 'B. 
1 CONT Er starb am Mittwoch, den 3. He died on Wednesday, 3 Aw, und wurde begraben Aw, and was buried 
1 CONT am Freitag im Jahre [5] 621 ndkZ On Friday in years [5] 621 ndkZ 
1 CONT (= 10.7.1861)* (= 10.7.1861) * 
1 CONT Seine Seele sei eingebunden im Bunde des Lebens. His soul was embedded in bundles of life. 
1 CONT
1 CONT *72 Jahre alt * 72 years old 
0 @N6000000006374281733@ NOTE Süßchen, daughter of David, wife of Jacob, son of Meier Wertheim from Rotenburg, died 14.3.1845 (Susette, geb. Ballin, Frau des Oelmüllers Jakob Wertheim aus Lispenhausen, 63 Jahre) 14.3.1845 (Susette, born Ballin, wife of Jacob W
1 CONC ertheim Oelmüllers from Lispenhausen, 63 years) 
0 @N6000000006374281634@ NOTE Meier, Sohn des Jakob Wertheim aus Lispenhausen, gest. 16.7.1871
0 @N6000000006374281718@ NOTE See Janet Akaha's email 12 Aug 2007 for family and descendants
0 @N6000000006374311879@ NOTE Übersetzung der hebräischen Inschrift:)
1 CONT
1 CONT Hier ruht
1 CONT die geachtete,
1 CONT mildtätige Frau, eine tugendhafte,
1 CONT tüchtige, gottesfürchtige
1 CONT Hausfrau, Frau Chaijle, Ehefrau des
1 CONT Me'ir Höch[ster]. Sie starb am 15.
1 CONT Iyyar [5] 652 n.d.k.Z. (= 4.5.1882).
1 CONT Ein guter Name ist besser als gutes Öl.
1 CONT Ihre Seele sei eingebunden im Bunde des Lebens. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rückseite:
1 CONT
1 CONT (Deutsche Inschrift:)
1 CONT
1 CONT Hier ruht in Gott
1 CONT Karoline Höchster
1 CONT von Storndorf
1 CONT starb d. 4. Mai 1882
1 CONT
1 CONT Karoline (Chaijle) Höchster, Ehefrau des Meier (Me'ir) Höchster zu Storndorf, gestorben am 04.05.1882. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ------------------------------------------
1 CONT Die Verstorbene war eine geborene Rosenthal und wurde etwa 1838 geboren; am 31.07.1863 heiratete sie Meier (Meyer) Höchster aus Storndorf, einen Sohn des Kaufmann Höchster [Grabnummer 23] und der Giedel geb. Herzberg, auch Herzber
1 CONC ger (www.jewishgen.org, Stand: 24.03.2009).
1 CONT Meier Höchster, geboren am 18.07.1839 in Storndorf, heiratete nach dem Tod Karolines in zweiter Ehe Zippora geb. Strauß, geboren am 18.11.1848 in Storndorf, Tochter des Juda Strauß und der Rebekka geb. Strauß. Mit seiner Familie z
1 CONC og er später nach Lauterbach. Dort starb Zippora am 06.09.1904, Meier am 12.04.1916. (Die Angaben dieses Absatzes nach Mathilda W. Stein: The Way It Was: The Jewish World of Rural Hesse, Atlanta/Georgia 2000, S. 265).
0 @N6000000006374303842@ NOTE 171. Teibchen Wertheim, geb. Levi aus Eschwege, gest. 22.2.1829 (Witwe des Meier Wertheim aus Rotenburg, 68 Jahre) 
0 @N6000000006374303831@ NOTE 65. Löser Wertheim, gest. 26.10.1859 (Leiser Wertheim, 65 Jahre) 
0 @N6000000006374373310@ NOTE Juspa, son of David Ballin sel from Rotenburg, died 15.6.1851 (son of David Heinemann Ballin Jettchen and his wife, born Ballin, 78 years) 
0 @N6000000006374281649@ NOTE 8. Minna Wertheim, geb. Heinemann, geb. 14.1.1828, gest. 11.6.1862, (Frau des Oelmüllers Meier Wertheim aus Lispenhausen, 35 Jahre)
1 CONT
1 CONT Minna value home, geb. Heinemann, geb. 14.1.1828, gest. 11.6.1862, (woman of oil Mueller Meier value home from Lispenhausen, 35 years)
1 CONT
1 CONT Hier ruht Here rests 
1 CONT Minna Wertheim Minna Wertheim 
1 CONT geb. Heinemann Born Heinemann 
1 CONT geb. am 14. Born on 14 Januar 1828 January 1828 
1 CONT gest. am 11.Juni 1862 died June 11, 1862 
1 CONT
1 CONT Friede ihrer Asche! Friede their ashes! 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Am 11. On 11 Juni 1862 verstarb Minna, Ehefrau des Oelmüllers Meier Minna died in June 1862, the wife of Oelmüllers Meier 
1 CONT Wertheim zu Lispenhausen, 35 Jahre alt. Wertheim to Lispenhausen, 35 years old. 
0 @N6000000006374281620@ NOTE WERTHEIM, HUGO (1854-1919), merchant and manufacturer, was born on 12 July 1854 at Lispenhausen, in the German electorate of Hesse-Kassel, son of Meyer Wertheim and his wife Minna, née Heinemann. Tall and dark, Hugo reached Melbou
1 CONC rne in the Great Britain in October 1875. Soon he was advertising, from premises at 39 Flinders Lane East, as agent for his father's cousin Joseph Wertheim, a well-established manufacturer of sewing machines. On 27 February 1882 H
1 CONC ugo was naturalized. Back in Germany, he married Joseph Wertheim's daughter Sophie Emilie (1864-1953) on 30 August 1885 at Frankfurt and the couple came to Melbourne. Thereafter, he frequently returned to Europe. In the 1880s he b
1 CONC uilt a 17-bedroomed mansion, Gotha (later Hadleigh Hall, demolished 1935), at South Yarra, where musicians such as the visiting Paderewski played.
1 CONT
1 CONT In a short time, with extensive advertising, Hugo established a substantial business, selling sewing machines, bicycles, pianos and other mechanical devices, under brands such as Wertheim, Electra, Planet, Griffin and Hapsburg. He
1 CONC  also mounted elaborate displays at agricultural shows and in 1901 at the Pan American Exposition, Buffalo, United States of America. O. C. Beale worked with him before setting up his own piano business in New South Wales. Hugo co
1 CONC ntinued to own 25 per cent of one of Beale's companies, which became Wertheim's Queensland business.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1908 at a cost of some £75,000, Wertheim opened a large, innovative piano factory at Richmond, Melbourne, intending to produce 2000 pianos and player pianos annually, predominantly using Australian materials. In laying the foun
1 CONC dation stone, Prime Minister Alfred Deakin, observed that 'few men with such opportunities for a life of ease would have embarked on such an enterprise . . . the first in Victoria to commence making pianos'. Theodore Fink remarked
1 CONC  that Wertheim had left behind the controversial position of an importer and grown into the patriotic position of a manufacturer. Premier Sir Thomas Bent, to reported cheers, said that he had found him as good a Briton as any in p
1 CONC romoting industry.
1 CONT
1 CONT World War I, however, caused difficulties for Wertheim. Australian military intelligence files showed that he and his family were reported numerous times as spies or 'pro-German'. Beginning in 1914, various wild assertions were in
1 CONC vestigated and found groundless. Wertheim's second son Rupert Carl (1892-1933), known as 'Soss' (for German sausage), served in France with the Australian Imperial Force and was thrice mentioned in dispatches; in September 1917 he
1 CONC  interrogated captured Germans, deriving information that averted a German counter-attack.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugo died of chronic hepatitis on 11 July 1919 at his home at South Yarra and left an estate in Victoria valued for probate at £51,539. His wife, two daughters and three sons survived him; Herbert Joseph (1886-1972), the eldest, c
1 CONC ontinued the business. Rupert became a sharebroker; he represented Victoria in inter-State tennis in 1913-27 and Australia in Davis Cup matches against Czechoslovakia in 1922. The piano factory closed in 1935, becoming a Heinz foo
1 CONC d processing plant and, in 1955, GTV Channel 9 studios and offices.
0 @N6000000006374311287@ NOTE According to "The Jewish World of Rural Hesse" by Mathilde Stein, Juda Strauss was Zippora's father. Pg 265
